## Quality gates (run before considering a task "done")
- For any PHP changes in the Synced Pattern Popups plugin, run WP-CLI Plugin Check against the plugin and fix all ERRORS before stopping.
  - Command (PowerShell): .\wp.ps1 plugin check synced-pattern-popups --path=.
- If Plugin Check reports warnings, address them when low-risk; otherwise add an inline justification (phpcs ignore) only when appropriate.
- After changes that affect frontend behavior (AJAX, modal JS/CSS), verify: modal opens, loads content, closes, and restores scroll/focus.

## WP-CLI workflow (prefer runtime verification over speculation)
- Prefer verifying WordPress/runtime facts via WP-CLI rather than assumptions.
  Examples:
  - .\wp.ps1 core version --path=.
  - .\wp.ps1 plugin list --path=.
  - .\wp.ps1 option get <option_name> --path=.
  - .\wp.ps1 post list --post_type=wp_block --path=.
- When debugging, use WP-CLI to reproduce minimal cases (e.g., fetch a specific wp_block post, render content, confirm meta).

## Abilities API + MCP Adapter workflow
- When implementing or changing “abilities”, always:
  - confirm Abilities API availability at runtime (WP version gating),
  - register abilities on the required actions,
  - provide at least one concrete test invocation per ability.
- Prefer testing abilities through the MCP Adapter interface when possible (list + execute) to validate the machine-readable contract, not just PHP output.

## WordPress coding + security defaults (must be applied)
- Always use capability checks for admin actions; nonces for any state change; sanitize input (wp_unslash + sanitize_*), and escape output (esc_html/esc_attr/wp_kses_post).
- For unauthenticated AJAX: validate input strictly, rate limit, and return generic errors (avoid information disclosure).
- Never enqueue editor-only styles on the frontend; explicitly filter editor handles if collecting dynamic assets.

## Caching invariants (avoid regressions)
- Any cache key prefix/group changes must include:
  - a cache-clear pathway (admin button already exists),
  - backward compatibility handling if old formats may exist,
  - a note on expected TTL behavior.
